<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gia Định</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gia Dinh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gia Định was the name of the Vietnamese settlement and citadel on the site of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Ho Chi Minh City</a>, and of the surrounding province of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyen Anh</a> took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> in 1788 and made it his base for the reconquest of the country. A French-designed citadel was built there in 1790, and the French captured it in 1859, making Saigon the capital of Cochinchina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gia-dinh.jpg" alt="Gia Định" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: trungydang · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cho_Ba_chieu_tp_hcm_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
